by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T06:51:12Z

Pushed a behaviour change on top, plus the paperwork. Worth a look before this goes further, @mp3000mp.

The four functions now throw when evaluated outside a request:

```
LogicException: The "is_granted()" function cannot be evaluated outside of a request.
```

The reason is `Assert\When`. In a console command or a Messenger worker there is no security context, and returning `false` there means the guarded constraints are silently skipped: validation passes and nothing reports why. `Assert\Expression` had the opposite problem, it produced a violation. Throwing makes both say the same thing, which is that the rule cannot be evaluated here.

Note this is deliberately keyed on the request, not on the token. A null token is normal inside a request, it just means nobody is logged in, and `is_granted()` must answer `false` for an anonymous visitor rather than blow up. `AuthorizationChecker::isGranted()` substitutes a `NullToken` for exactly that case. So the provider now takes a `RequestStack` and only throws when there is no main request. `testTheFunctionsReturnFalseWithoutAnyToken` covers the anonymous case and is the regression guard for it.

Nothing here is a BC break: `is_granted()` in a validator expression is a `SyntaxError` on every released branch, so there is no existing behaviour to preserve. That also means the choice is free, and if you or the security team prefer `false` over throwing, say so and I will revert this part.

The rest is paperwork:

- moved the CHANGELOG entry from the released 8.1 section to 8.2
- `testIsGrantedReturnsFalseWithoutAnyToken` used a stubbed `AccessDecisionManagerInterface`, which returns `false` whatever the token is, so it passed with a token too. It now uses a real `AccessDecisionManager` with `AuthenticatedVoter` and `RoleVoter`, covers all four functions, and fails if the functions stop denying.
- added `AddValidatorSecurityExpressionLanguageProviderPassTest`, covering both services present, security absent, and validator absent. The wiring had no test at all.

One thing for the description rather than the code: it says the `validator.expression_language_provider` id stays free for applications. That id is already `Validator\Constraints\ExpressionLanguageProvider`, and defining it drops `is_valid()` from the function list. Worth correcting before the docs PR picks it up.
